            <ArticleTitle>[Internal strontium-89 radiotherapy for malignant bony metastasis].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>507-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This work was done to evaluate the indication, effectiveness, and side effects of internal radiotherapy with radioactive nuclide strontium-89 (89Sr) in patients with malignant metastasis in the bone.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Fifty-six patients with skeletal metastasis received this internal radiotherapy. The patients were observed and followed up with respect to pain control, lesion improvement and side effects.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The overall effective rate of pain control was 76.8% with the effective rate of prostatic cancer and breast cancer higher than 80%. The lesions in 81.8% patients as assessed by SPECT imaging, were improved. The mild lowering of white cells, platelets and red cells was the main side effect.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Internal radiotherapy with 89Sr is very useful for patients with malignant cancer metastasis in the bone.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
